+- **Precision**: Standard floating-point precision issues may arise in intersection tests, though not explicitly detailed in sources [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of nodes visited and the cost of intersection tests. Coherent traversal reduces the number of node visits per packet compared to single-ray traversal, but packet overhead exists. LOD reduces the depth of traversal. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The octree structure is stored in CPU main memory. Lossless compression reduces the footprint compared to the original dataset. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **Performance:** Outperforms single-ray ray tracing of the same large data on distributed clusters. Achieves interactive performance on laptops and commodity desktops. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **Strengths:** Interactive performance on commodity hardware; smooth gradient normals; shadows; lower memory footprint. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **Limitations:** Scalability issues with coherent traversal on hierarchical structures; low coherence in large volumes from far distances; potential for more intersection tests than single-ray if not optimized. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **When to Use:** Interactive visualization of large volumetric datasets on commodity CPUs where memory and performance are constraints. [S1, unofficial]
-*   **When Not to Use:** When high spatial coherence is not present (e.g., very distant camera views) or when exact high-resolution detail is required without LOD smoothing. [S1, unofficial]
+- **Time Complexity**: Depends on the number of nodes visited per packet. With LOD, the depth of traversal is limited, reducing complexity. Without LOD, it can be higher than single-ray due to packet overhead [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: The octree structure is stored in CPU main memory. The multiresolution representation allows for a smaller footprint than uncompressed data [S1, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance**: The system outperforms ray tracing the same large data on a distributed cluster on a multicore workstation [S1, unofficial]. It achieves interactive performance on laptops and commodity desktops [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths**: Interactive performance on commodity hardware, smooth gradients due to LOD smoothing, lower memory footprint [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: Scalability issues with coherent traversal, potential performance loss without LOD, low coherence in distant views [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to Use**: Interactive visualization of large volumes on commodity CPUs where smoothness is acceptable and hardware is limited [S1, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use**: When maximum precision is required at all scales, or when the scene has very high coherence without LOD (single-ray might be simpler) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was developed by Knoll et al. and presented in the thesis "Ray Tracing Implicit Surfaces for Interactive Visualization" (2006) and the paper "Coherent Multiresolution Isosurface Ray Tracing" [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the coherent kd-tree isosurface ray tracer of Wald et al. and extends it to octree volumes with multiresolution LODs. The work appeared in Chapter 4 of the thesis and in the cited paper [60]. [S1, unofficial]
